    h1: 'Google Dorking Guide: Master Advanced Search for OSINT Investigation',
    description: 'Learn Google dorking — advanced search operators that help OSINT investigators find exposed files, databases, login pages, and sensitive information indexed by search engines.',
    intro: 'Google dorking (also called Google hacking) is the practice of using advanced search operators to find specific types of information indexed by search engines. Standard searches return a broad set of results. With the right combination of operators, investigators can surface exposed documents, vulnerable systems, leaked credentials, camera feeds, and other sensitive data that is technically public because it is indexed. This guide covers the essential Google operators, practical search patterns, and responsible use guidelines.',
    sections: [
      { h2: 'Core Google Search Operators', content: 'The foundation of Google dorking is a small set of operators. `site:` restricts results to a specific domain (e.g., site:example.com finds all indexed pages on that domain). `filetype:` returns files of a specific type (filetype:pdf finds PDFs, filetype:xls finds spreadsheets). `intitle:` searches the page title only. `inurl:` searches within URLs. `intext:` searches within page body content. `cache:` shows Google\'s cached version of a page. `link:` finds pages that link to a URL (now deprecated in most search engines). These operators become powerful when combined — for example: `site:gov.br filetype:pdf "relatorio" 2024` finds PDF reports on Brazilian government domains.' },
      { h2: 'Finding Exposed Files and Databases', content: 'One of the most common dorking applications is finding accidentally exposed files. Search patterns like `intitle:"index of" "backup"` find open directory listings. `filetype:env "DB_PASSWORD"` can surface accidentally committed environment files. `filetype:sql "create table"` finds exposed database dumps. `filetype:log "error" site:example.com` surfaces error logs. These searches demonstrate how poor server configuration can make sensitive files publicly accessible. Security researchers use these techniques to identify and report exposures. System administrators use them to audit their own infrastructure for accidental disclosure.' },
      { h2: 'Login Pages, Admin Panels, and Camera Feeds', content: 'Specific dork patterns reliably surface login pages and administrative interfaces. `inurl:"/admin/login"` and `intitle:"Administrator Login"` are common patterns. Camera feeds are particularly well-documented: `inurl:"/view.shtml"` and `intitle:"Live View / - AXIS"` expose network cameras. CCTV systems from specific manufacturers have predictable URL patterns that dorks can identify. While accessing these systems without authorisation is illegal, identifying their existence from search results is lawful. Security professionals use this knowledge to help clients audit their exposed assets.' },
      { h2: 'Combining Operators for OSINT Investigations', content: 'The real power of Google dorking comes from combining operators. To find LinkedIn profiles for employees at a company: `site:linkedin.com "Company Name" "Senior Engineer"`. To find conference presentations about a topic: `site:slideshare.net filetype:pptx "threat intelligence" 2023`. To find court documents: `site:pacer.gov OR site:courtlistener.com "defendant" "Company Name"`. To find a person\'s social accounts: `"firstname lastname" site:twitter.com OR site:instagram.com OR site:github.com`. These patterns can be refined iteratively by adding or removing operators until the results focus on what you need.' },
      { h2: 'Bing, DuckDuckGo, and Other Search Engines', content: 'While Google is the most powerful search engine for dorking, other engines index different content. Bing\'s `contains:` operator finds pages with links to specific file types. DuckDuckGo does not support the full operator set but can be useful for less-censored results. Yandex indexes significant amounts of Russian-language content and can return different results for international investigations. Baidu is essential for research involving Chinese-language content. Brave Search provides an independent index. Using multiple search engines in combination gives the most complete picture of what is publicly indexed.' },
      { h2: 'Responsible and Legal Use', content: 'Google dorking is a dual-use technique. Used responsibly — to audit your own systems, conduct authorised security assessments, or research publicly available information — it is a legitimate and powerful tool. Using dorks to access systems you are not authorised to access, to harvest credentials for misuse, or to violate platform terms of service crosses legal lines in most jurisdictions. The Google Hacking Database (GHDB) maintained by Exploit-DB documents thousands of dork patterns and is an essential reference. Always ensure your use of dorking is lawful, ethical, and within the scope of any engagement you are conducting.' },
    ],
