    slug: 'network-scanning-tools',
    title: 'Network Scanning Tools: Nmap, Masscan & Internet Reconnaissance',
    h1: 'Network Scanning Tools for Security Research and Asset Discovery',
    description: 'A practical guide to network scanning tools including Nmap, Masscan, RustScan, and internet-scale scanners like Shodan and Censys for security research and OSINT.',
    intro: 'Network scanning is the process of discovering hosts, services, and vulnerabilities on a network or across the public internet. For security researchers, penetration testers, and OSINT investigators, network scanning tools are fundamental to understanding attack surface and infrastructure. This guide covers the most important tools — from local network scanners to planet-scale internet intelligence platforms — and how to use them effectively and legally.',
    sections: [
      { h2: 'Nmap: The Standard Network Scanner', content: 'Nmap (Network Mapper) is the industry-standard tool for network exploration and security auditing. It can discover hosts, scan ports, detect operating systems, identify running services, and execute custom scripts from its NSE (Nmap Scripting Engine) library. A basic scan of a target host runs as `nmap -sV -O target.com`. The -sV flag enables service version detection; -O attempts OS fingerprinting. Nmap\'s stealth scan (-sS) sends SYN packets without completing the TCP handshake, reducing log entries. Nmap should only be run against networks and hosts you own or have explicit written authorisation to test. Unauthorised scanning is illegal in most jurisdictions.' },
      { h2: 'Masscan and RustScan: High-Speed Port Discovery', content: 'Masscan is capable of scanning the entire IPv4 address space in under six minutes on high-bandwidth connections. It is designed purely for speed at the cost of accuracy — it typically delivers false positives that must be validated with Nmap. RustScan is a modern alternative written in Rust that performs fast port discovery and then hands off confirmed open ports to Nmap for detailed enumeration. Both tools are useful for rapid initial discovery in large-scope authorised assessments. For internet-scale OSINT research, these tools should be used against your own infrastructure or through research programmes — never against third-party targets without authorisation.' },
      { h2: 'Shodan, Censys, and Internet-Scale Intelligence', content: 'Rather than scanning directly, many OSINT investigations use passive internet intelligence platforms that have already scanned the entire public internet and indexed the results. Shodan indexes banners from billions of internet-connected devices and is searchable by IP, hostname, port, banner content, and geographic location. Censys provides similar coverage with strong certificate and protocol analysis. Criminal IP combines threat intelligence with asset discovery. Netlas and BinaryEdge offer alternative indexes. These platforms let researchers explore internet infrastructure without conducting active scans, making them suitable for passive OSINT against third-party targets.' },
      { h2: 'DNS Reconnaissance and Subdomain Enumeration', content: 'Before port scanning, DNS reconnaissance maps the target\'s internet footprint. Tools like Subfinder and Amass enumerate subdomains by querying DNS resolvers, scraping certificate transparency logs (via crt.sh), and checking passive DNS databases. DNSx validates discovered subdomains. dnsTwist identifies typosquat domains that could be used for phishing. SecurityTrails provides historical DNS data showing how a domain\'s infrastructure has changed over time. A complete DNS enumeration gives investigators the full scope of an organisation\'s internet-facing infrastructure — far more than the primary domain alone reveals.' },
      { h2: 'Application-Layer Scanning: Nikto and Nuclei', content: 'Once open ports and services are discovered, application-layer scanners check for known vulnerabilities. Nikto is a web server scanner that checks for thousands of potentially dangerous files, server misconfigurations, and outdated software versions. Nuclei is a highly flexible template-based scanner from ProjectDiscovery — the same team behind Subfinder, Httpx, and Katana. Nuclei templates are community-maintained and cover CVEs, misconfigurations, exposed panels, and custom checks. Both tools are designed for authorised security assessments and should never be used against targets without explicit written permission from the system owner.' },
      { h2: 'Legal and Ethical Framework for Network Scanning', content: 'Network scanning law varies by jurisdiction but most countries treat unauthorised scanning as a criminal act under computer fraud statutes. In the United States, the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) has been interpreted to cover unauthorised port scanning. In the EU, the Computer Misuse Act equivalents apply. Always obtain written, scope-limited authorisation before scanning any network you do not own. For OSINT purposes, use passive intelligence platforms (Shodan, Censys) to investigate third-party infrastructure rather than conducting active scans. Responsible disclosure programmes exist to report security issues found through legitimate research.' },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Is Nmap scanning illegal?', a: 'Scanning your own networks is legal. Scanning others without authorisation is illegal in most countries under computer fraud laws.' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between Shodan and Nmap?', a: 'Nmap actively scans target hosts you direct it at. Shodan passively queries a pre-built index of internet devices that Shodan\'s own scanners have already probed.' },
      { q: 'Can I scan public IP addresses legally?', a: 'Generally no — public IP addresses are assigned to specific owners and scanning them constitutes unauthorised access under most legal frameworks.' },
      { q: 'What is passive network reconnaissance?', a: 'Passive reconnaissance discovers information about a target without directly interacting with their systems — using Shodan, DNS records, certificate transparency logs, and WHOIS data.' },
    ],
